      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. INTRODUCTION TO MODAL VERBS"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Modal auxiliary verbs express ability, possibility, permission, or obligation and are followed by the base form of the verb (V1).",
        "Modals never change their form regardless of the subject's person or number (no -s, -ed, or -ing forms).",
        "Modal Perfect constructions (Modal + have + V3) express past unfulfilled actions, regrets, or past logical deductions.",
        "Marginal auxiliary verbs like 'Need' and 'Dare' can function both as main verbs and as modal verbs in negative/interrogative contexts.",
        "'Ought to' and 'Used to' are the only standard modals that require the infinitive marker 'to'."
      ],
      "id": "0z9pq7h"
    },
